  "answer_text": "**Hipparchus of Nicaea** (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, geographer, and mathematician who is widely considered one of the greatest scientific minds of antiquity. While many of his original writings have been lost, historical records (primarily from Ptolemy) credit him with inventing several foundational tools, systems, and mathematical concepts. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what Hipparchus invented and developed:\n\n### 1. Trigonometry (and the Table of Chords)\nHipparchus is widely celebrated as the **\"father of trigonometry.\"** To solve complex astronomical problems, he developed the first systematic trigonometric tables. \n* He created a **Table of Chords** (a precursor to the modern sine function) in a treatise spanning 12 books. \n* This invention allowed astronomers to calculate the relationship between the angles and sides of a triangle, transforming astronomy from a purely descriptive study into a highly quantitative science.\n\n### 2. The Planispheric Astrolabe\nHipparchus is traditionally credited with inventing\u2014or at least providing the mathematical foundation for\u2014the **planispheric astrolabe**. \n* An astrolabe is a portable \"analog computer\" used to solve astronomical problems, predict the positions of stars and the Sun, and tell the time. \n* Hipparchus mastered the math of **stereographic projection** (the geometric method of projecting a 3D celestial sphere onto a 2D flat plane), which is the core principle behind the device.\n\n### 3. The Hipparchan Dioptra\nTo measure celestial angles with high precision, Hipparchus invented a specialized sighting instrument called a **dioptra**. \n* His design featured a long, graduated four-foot rod equipped with a fixed sighting eyelet at one end and a sliding vane (pinnule) at the other. \n* By sliding the vane until it perfectly obscured the Moon or Sun, Hipparchus could mathematically calculate the apparent angular diameter of these celestial bodies. This is considered a precursor to the Jacob\u2019s staff.\n\n### 4. The Armillary Sphere\nWhile there is some debate about whether he invented it from scratch or heavily refined a basic model, Hipparchus is widely credited with developing the **armillary sphere**. This instrument features a spherical framework of rings representing lines of celestial latitude and longitude. He used this tool to track the movements of the planets and map the stars.\n\n### 5. The Stellar Magnitude Scale\nWhen compiling his star catalog, Hipparchus invented a way to classify the brightness of stars. \n* He created a **six-tier brightness scale** (stellar magnitude), labeling the brightest stars he could see as \"first magnitude\" (1st class) and the faintest visible stars as \"sixth magnitude.\"\n* This basic classification scale, though refined mathematically, is still used by modern astronomers.\n\n### 6. The Geographical Coordinate Grid (Latitude & Longitude)\nHipparchus was the first to apply a systematic grid of **latitude and longitude** to the entire known world. \n* He proposed dividing the Earth into 360 degrees and suggested using astronomical events (like lunar eclipses observed from different locations simultaneously) to calculate precise differences in longitude. \n\n---\n\n### Other Landmark Discoveries:\nThough not \"physical inventions,\" Hipparchus used his custom tools to make massive scientific breakthroughs:\n* **Precession of the Equinoxes:** By comparing his star charts with observations made by older Babylonian and Greek astronomers, he discovered that the Earth\u2019s rotational axis slowly shifts in direction over a 26,000-year cycle. \n* **The First Western Star Catalog:** He cataloged the positions and brightness of approximately 850 to 1,000 stars, mapping them onto a precise grid.\n* **Calculating the Distance to the Moon:** Using trigonometry and observations of solar eclipses, he calculated the distance between the Earth and the Moon with incredible accuracy for his time.",
